==English==
{{was wotd|2025|April|28}}

===Etymology===
{{multiple images
|direction = vertical
|image1 = The British Army in North Africa 1942 E18640.jpg
|caption1 = A [[soldier#Noun|soldier]] of the {{w|British Army}} [[refuel]]ling a {{w|Crusader tank}} from a flimsy ''(noun {{senseno|en|container}})'' in October 1942 during the {{w|North African Campaign}}.
}}

The origin of the {{glossary|adjective}} is {{uncertain|en|nocap=1}}; it is possibly from {{m|en|flim-flam|flim(-flam)|(''noun'') false information presented as true, misinformation, nonsense; poor attempt at deception, confidence trick, pretence; (''adjective'') frivolous, nonsensical; deceptive; fictitious}}<ref>{{R:OED Online|entry=flim-flam|pos=n.'' and ''adj|id=4512421176|date=December 2024|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|entry=flimflam|pos=n}}</ref><ref>{{R:Lexico|pos=adj.'' and ''n}}</ref> or a {{metathesis|en|film|t=thin layer of a substance; slender thread}} + {{affix|en|-sy|pos1={{glossary|suffix}} forming adjectives and nouns}}.<ref name="OED">Compare {{R:OED Online|pos=adj.'' and ''n|id=1132774188|date=December 2024}}</ref>

The {{glossary|noun}}<ref name="OED"/> and {{glossary|verb}}<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=4707089132|date=December 2024}}</ref> are derived from the noun. Noun {{senseno|en|container}} (“metal container”) refers to the fact that the containers often split along their seams and leaked.

