===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er|more}}

# [[likely#Adjective|Likely]] to [[bend#Verb|bend]] or [[break#Verb|break]] under [[pressure#Noun|pressure]]; [[easily]] [[damage#Verb|damaged]]; [[frail#Adjective|frail]], [[unsubstantial]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#: {{synonyms|en|fragile|shaky|weak}}
#: {{antonyms|en|robust|strong|sturdy|unflimsy}}
#: {{ux|en|He expected the '''flimsy''' structure to collapse at any moment.}}
#* {{RQ:Sheridan Rivals|page=20|passage=Yet do I carry every vvhere vvith me ſuch a confounded farago of doubts, fears, hopes, vviſhes, and all the '''flimſy''' furniture of a country Miſs's brain!}}
#* {{RQ:Cowper Poems|poem=Retirement|page=290|passage=But reveries (for human minds vvill act) / Specious in ſhovv, impoſſible in fact, / Thoſe '''flimſy''' vvebs that break as ſoon as vvrought, / Attain not to the dignity of thought.}}
#* {{RQ:Shelley Queen Mab|page=60|passage=Compelled, by its deformity, to screen / With '''flimsy''' veil of justice and of right, / Its unattractive lineaments, that scare / All, save the brood of ignorance: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Henry Esmond|volume=II|chapter=Recapitulations|page=64|passage=[T]here always comes a day when the rouſed publick indignation kicks their '''flimſy''' edifice down, and ſends its cowardly enemies a-flying.}}
## Of [[clothing#Noun|clothing]]: very [[light#Adjective|light]] and [[thin#Adjective|thin]].
##: {{synonyms|en|diaphanous|filmy|gossamer|gossamer-thin|gossamery}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}}
## Of an [[argument#Noun|argument]], [[explanation]], etc.: [[ill-founded]], [[unconvincing#Adjective|unconvincing]], [[weak]]; also, [[unimportant]]; [[paltry]], [[trivial#Adjective|trivial]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
##: {{synonyms|en|feeble|unfounded|unsubstantiated}}
##: {{antonyms|en|well-founded|substantiated}}
##: {{ux|en|a '''flimsy''' excuse&emsp;&emsp;&emsp; the '''flimsiest''' of theories}}
##* {{RQ:Pope Arbuthnot|lines=87–90|page=5|passage=VVho ſhames a Scribler? break one cobvveb thro', / He ſpins the ſlight, ſelf-pleaſing thread anevv; / Deſtroy his Fib, or Sophiſtry; in vain, / The Creature's at his dirty vvork again; / Thron'd in the Centre of his thin deſigns; / Proud of a vaſt Extent of '''flimzy''' lines.|footer=The scribbler is likened to a spider, and the lines he writes to flimsy threads of cobwebs.}}
##* {{RQ:Blackstone Commentaries|book=I|chapter=Of the King, and His Title|page=194|passage=And hovvever '''flimzey''' this title, and thoſe of VVilliam Rufus {{quote-gloss|{{w|William II of England}}}} and Stephen of Blois {{quote-gloss|{{w|Stephen, King of England}}}}, may appear at this diſtance to us, after the lavv of deſcents hath novv been ſettled for ſo many centuries, they vvere ſufficient to puzzle the underſtandings of our brave, but unlettered, anceſtors.}}
## Of a [[person#Noun|person]]: [[lack#Verb|lacking]] [[depth]] of [[character]] or [[understanding#Noun|understanding]]; [[frivolous]], [[superficial#Adjective|superficial]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
##: {{synonyms|en|flighty|silly|trifling|unserious}}
##: {{antonyms|en|serious|thoughtful}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Canongate|volume=II|page=305|passage="Yes, fell woman," answered Middlemas; "but was it I who encouraged the young tyrant's outrageous passion for a portrait, or who formed the abominable plan of placing the original within his power?" / "No—for to do so required brain and wit. But it was thine, '''flimsy''' villain, to execute the device which a bolder genius planned; it was thine to entice the woman to this foreign shore, under pretence of a love, which, on thy part, cold-blooded miscreant, never had existed."}}
##* {{RQ:Hunt Men Women|volume=I|chapter=Duchess of St. Albans|page=195|passage=Poor, '''flimsy''', wise, foolish, aristocratical, old-bachelor {{w|Horace Walpole}}, is shocked at his nephew {{quote-gloss|{{w|George Walpole, 3rd Earl of Orford}}?}} marrying an actress who brought him good children, {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} Of a person, their [[physical#Adjective|physical]] [[makeup#Noun|makeup]], or their [[health]]: [[delicate#Adjective|delicate]], frail. {{defdate|18th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Walpole Letters to Mann|volume=I|date=7 January 1742|letter=XVIII|page=72|passage={{...}} I have a very '''flimsy''' constitution, consequently the young women won't taste my wit, and it is a long while before wit makes its own way in the world; especially, as I never prove it, by assuring people that I have it by me.}}

