===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}} {{term-label|en|dated|or|historical}}

# {{senseid|en|paper}}{{lb|en|also|attributive|uncountable}} [[thin#Adjective|Thin]] [[typing#Noun|typing]] [[paper#Noun|paper]] [[use#Verb|used]] together with [[carbon paper]] in a [[typewriter]] to [[make#Verb|make]] [[multiple#Adjective|multiple]] [[copy#Noun|copies]] of a [[document#Noun|document]]; {{lb|en|countable}} a [[sheet#Noun|sheet]] of such paper.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1977|author=w:John Le Carré|title=The Honourable Schoolboy|page=251|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=2010|passage=Smiley peered once more at the '''flimsy''' which he still clutched in his pudgy hand.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A document [[print#Verb|printed]] or [[type#Verb|typed]] on such paper.
## {{lb|en|naval slang|countable}} A [[service#Noun|service]] [[certificate#Noun|certificate]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1964|author=Australia. Parliament|title=Records of the Proceedings and Printed Papers of the Parliament|passage=A perusal of the comments of officers under whom he has served as recorded in his “'''flimsies'''" indicates that he has almost consistently received high commendation for his service.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1994|author=John Wells|title=The Royal Navy: An Illustrated Social History, 1870-1982|page=7|passage=Regulations required a commanding officer to render annual confidential reports on the character and ability of his officers - with particular reference to sobriety - on forms known as ''''flimsies''''.}}
## {{lb|en|slang|countable}} A [[banknote]]; {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[paper money]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1846|author=George William MacArthur Reynolds|title=The Mysteries of London|page=60|passage={{...}} Q was a Queer-screen, that served as a blind; / R was a Reader, with '''flimsies''' well lined; {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|newspapers|uncountable}} The [[text#Noun|text]] to be [[set#Verb|set]] into [[page#Noun|pages]] of [[magazine]]s, [[newspaper#Noun|newspapers]], etc.; [[copy#Noun|copy]].
# {{senseid|en|container}}{{lb|en|UK|military slang|historical}} A [[hexahedral]] [[metal#Adjective|metal]] [[container]] with a [[capacity]] of [[four#Numeral|four]] [[imperial gallon]]s (about 18 [[litre]]s) used by the {{w|British Army}} during [[World War II]] to [[hold#Verb|hold]] [[fuel#Noun|fuel]].

